1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:07,106
A developer's tour of the What-If Tool: its API, the JSON in and out, the database, the AI layer,

2
00:00:07,106 --> 00:00:08,525
and how to extend it.

3
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:10,496
Two flows.

4
00:00:10,496 --> 00:00:16,137
Your systems post JSON to the backend API, which validates it, computes what it can,

5
00:00:16,137 --> 00:00:18,017
and stores it in PostgreSQL.

6
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:24,463
The browser runs the React app: it reads results over REST, and calls the language model itself,

7
00:00:24,463 --> 00:00:27,551
through an nginx proxy, to Ollama on the host.

8
00:00:27,551 --> 00:00:29,700
The backend never calls a model.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,103
Every endpoint is documented live at slash swagger: three domains, education,

10
00:00:36,103 --> 00:00:41,868
yard logistics and check-in logs, with schemas from the code and a curl for every call.

11
00:00:41,868 --> 00:00:46,175
There's no authentication yet, so put it behind your own gateway.

12
00:00:47,475 --> 00:00:51,101
Yard data goes in exactly as the simulator wrote it.

13
00:00:51,101 --> 00:00:56,609
You add a case name, and per run an id and a label; the export is stored as is.

14
00:00:56,609 --> 00:01:01,350
Later runs go to the case's runs endpoint, a natural webhook target.

15
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:10,334
The backend checks only the four parts it reads, and rejects anything else with a 400 that says what's wrong.

16
00:01:10,334 --> 00:01:13,225
A body that breaks the schema gets a 422.

17
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:21,309
For education, a what-if is a named list of typed adjustments, posted to a case's scenarios endpoint.

18
00:01:21,309 --> 00:01:27,421
They apply in a fixed order: assignment and exam changes, a schedule rebuild, then lecture,

19
00:01:27,421 --> 00:01:29,100
lab and homework changes.

20
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,365
Every result is an audit trail.

21
00:01:32,365 --> 00:01:37,247
Here every adjustment applied, but the peak still rose, so improved is false.

22
00:01:37,247 --> 00:01:43,650
A two-oh-one means it ran, not that it helped: check improved, and each adjustment's status and code.

23
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:52,299
In PostgreSQL, partner data is stored as JSON B exactly as sent, so new fields never need a migration.

24
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:58,135
Derived numbers are computed once at ingest; cheap views are rebuilt per request.

25
00:01:58,135 --> 00:02:03,250
Schema changes go in the SQL file, the Drizzle schema, and a migration.

26
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:10,979
Every model call comes from the browser: a feature calls a React hook, which calls one file,

27
00:02:10,979 --> 00:02:15,800
ollama service dot T S, then an nginx proxy, then Ollama on the host.

28
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:23,235
Here's the logs chat request: model, streaming, a low temperature, a reply cap, and messages.

29
00:02:23,235 --> 00:02:28,975
The system message is rebuilt from page data every time; nothing is stored server-side.

30
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:32,333
Three ways to swap the model.

31
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:35,029
Any Ollama model: pull it and pick it.

32
00:02:35,029 --> 00:02:38,647
An Ollama-compatible server: point the proxy at it.

33
00:02:38,647 --> 00:02:43,756
OpenAI-style or hosted APIs: write an adapter in ollama service dot T S.

34
00:02:43,756 --> 00:02:50,000
And never put an API key in a VITE variable; keep it server-side, behind authentication.

35
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:53,768
Three recipes cover most extensions.

36
00:02:53,768 --> 00:02:57,059
A new use case: copy the logs domain end to end.

37
00:02:57,059 --> 00:03:00,349
A new adjustment type: adjustments and simulate.

38
00:03:00,349 --> 00:03:06,725
A new proposal change: its type, validation, and the prompt's schema, or the parser drops it.

39
00:03:08,025 --> 00:03:14,137
To integrate, push each finished run to the runs endpoint and read results with plain gets; the

40
00:03:14,137 --> 00:03:16,646
example bodies double as test fixtures.

41
00:03:16,646 --> 00:03:22,436
Watch out: Ollama must allow the app's origin, there's no authentication and CORS is open,

42
00:03:22,436 --> 00:03:25,975
and sending proposals to the simulator isn't built yet.

43
00:03:27,275 --> 00:03:31,301
Every endpoint, contract and recipe is in the developer guide.

44
00:03:31,301 --> 00:03:32,600
Thanks for watching.
